<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/condo-building.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/condo-building-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/condo-building.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/claim-dispute-services/">Services</a><span>/</span>Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</nav><p class="kicker lt">Claim dispute service</p><h1>Commercial Insurance Appraisal in South Carolina</h1><p class="lede">Large buildings, shared roofs and several policies at once make commercial claims harder to price and easier to underpay. We represent associations, congregations and property owners in appraisal across the state.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn cu" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ol" href="/contact-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts"><div><small>Best for</small><b>Boards, owners, managers</b></div><div><small>Property types</small><b>HOA, condo, church, retail</b></div><div><small>Watch for</small><b>Coinsurance &amp; deductibles</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Where commercial insurance appraisal in South Carolina fits</h2>
<p>Commercial insurance appraisal in South Carolina follows the same basic structure as a homeowners appraisal: two appraisers, an umpire if needed, and a binding amount once two of the three sign. What changes is the scale. A single hurricane or hail event can touch dozens of roofs, hundreds of units and several overlapping policies, and small pricing differences multiply quickly.</p>
<p>We work with homeowners associations, condominium regimes, churches, retail and office owners, and apartment communities, from Grand Strand high-rises to Upstate shopping centers and Midlands garden-style apartments.</p>
<h2>Condo master policies versus HO-6 policies</h2>
<p>In most condominium regimes the association’s master policy covers the building structure and common elements, while each owner’s HO-6 policy covers interior finishes, improvements and personal property. Exactly where that line falls depends on the master deed, the bylaws and the policies themselves.</p>
<p>That boundary matters in appraisal. If the master carrier treats drywall or cabinets as owner items and the HO-6 carrier treats them as building items, nobody pays for them. We map each scope item to the policy that should carry it before the panel starts.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Board decisions</b>An association usually needs a board vote, and sometimes a manager or attorney’s sign-off, before demanding appraisal. We can provide a written summary of the gap for board members to review.</div>
<h2>Churches, retail and multifamily properties</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Churches.</strong> Steeples, stained glass, vaulted ceilings, pipe organs and fellowship halls need specialty trades and careful pricing. Many congregations also run schools or daycare spaces with their own code requirements.</li>
<li><strong>Retail and office.</strong> Low-slope TPO, modified bitumen and built-up roofs, storefront glazing, rooftop units and tenant build-outs, often with lease terms that split responsibility.</li>
<li><strong>Multifamily.</strong> Repeated unit layouts, breezeways, stair towers and long runs of vinyl siding or shingles where matching and access drive the cost.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Coinsurance, deductibles and other commercial disputes</h2>
<p>Many commercial policies include a coinsurance clause. If the building was insured for less than the required percentage of its value at the time of loss, the payment can be reduced in proportion. The appraisal award sets the amount of loss and, depending on wording, sometimes values that bear on the coinsurance calculation, so that number gets close attention.</p>
<p>Other frequent disputes include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Percentage named-storm or wind deductibles applied per building versus per occurrence.</li>
<li>Code upgrades for commercial buildings, including fire separation and accessibility items triggered by larger repairs.</li>
<li>Overhead and profit and general conditions on multi-trade projects.</li>
<li>Whether older roofs were already failing before the storm.</li>
<li>Business income and extra expense, which some panels include and others leave out.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Records that help a commercial appraisal</h2>
<p>Useful records include the full policy with every endorsement and schedule of locations, the statement of values, prior roof reports and maintenance records, building plans, reserve studies for associations, invoices for mitigation and temporary repairs, and contractor proposals. For associations, minutes showing when damage was reported help the timeline.</p>
<h2>How we organize a multi-building panel</h2>
<p>For associations and apartment communities, we usually build the estimate building by building, with a summary that rolls up to the full claim. That makes it easier for the other appraiser to agree on whole buildings and for an umpire, if needed, to focus on the few that remain in dispute. It also helps boards and managers explain the result to owners and lenders.</p>
<h2>When commercial appraisal is the wrong tool</h2>
<p>If the insurer is relying on an exclusion, a vacancy clause, a protective safeguards condition or late notice, the dispute is about coverage and may need a South Carolina attorney. Disagreements among owners and the association about who must pay are governance questions, not appraisal questions. And if the claim is split between an SCWHUA wind policy and another carrier, each policy’s process runs on its own track. See <a href="/appraisal-guides/wind-pool-claims-and-appraisal/">our guide to wind pool claims</a> for more.</p>

<h2>Questions about commercial &amp; hoa appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can an HOA and individual unit owners appraise at the same time?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes, but the claims are separate. The association appraises under the master policy and each owner under their own HO-6, so coordinating the scopes helps avoid gaps.</p></div></details><details><summary>What is a coinsurance penalty?</summary><div class="a"><p>It is a reduction in payment when a building was insured for less than the percentage of value the policy requires. How it is calculated depends on the policy wording.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal include business income losses?</summary><div class="a"><p>Sometimes. It depends on the policy and on whether the demand covers time-element losses. We confirm that with the other appraiser before the panel starts.</p></div></details></div>
